# newdoc id = GUM_court_mitigation
# global.Entity = eid-etype-head-other-infstat-centering-minspan-link-identity
# meta::author = Supreme Court of Ohio
# meta::dateCollected = 2023-08-31
# meta::dateCreated = 2023-06-27
# meta::dateModified = 2023-06-27
# meta::genre = court
# meta::salientEntities = 6 (5*), 14 (5*), 15 (5*), 22 (5*), 25 (5*), 5 (4*), 23 (4), 3 (3*), 17 (3*), 21 (2*), 84 (2*), 1 (1*), 13 (1), 19 (1*), 20 (1), 27 (1), 59 (1)
# meta::sourceURL = https://ohiochannel.org/video/supreme-court-of-ohio-case-no-2022-1037-state-v-hurt
# meta::speakerCount = 3
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Attorney Eric Levi, representing appellant Darnell Hurt before Chief Justice Kennedy and the Court, argues that his client, charged for felonious assault and felony murder, should not be retried, since a prior jury already applied a provocation mitigator for voluntary manslaughter to him, and therefore effectively acquitted him of being prosecuted based on 'double jeopardy'.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) Attorney Eric Levi argues before the Court that collateral estoppel should prevent his client Darnell Hurt from being retried for murder charges since a jury previously found in his favor regarding provocation mitigators in a voluntary manslaughter case.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) Eric Levi argues that under collateral estoppel and double jeopardy principles, Darnell Hurt should not face retrial for felony murder due to a previous jury finding a provocation mitigator, which acts as an affirmative defense, and contends the Ohio court should rule before retrial to prevent Hurt from facing the same charges again.
# meta::summary4 = (Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct) Eric Levi argues that collateral estoppel prevents retrial of Darnell Hurt, whose prior jury found provocation mitigators, and that this issue is ripe for ruling prior to a new trial.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) Appellant Darnell Hurt's legal team argues that the previous jury's finding regarding the provocation mitigator for voluntary manslaughter should be binding due to collateral estoppel, preventing the need to re-prove the same mitigating circumstances in a retrial for additional charges.
# meta::title = Supreme Court of Ohio - Case No. 2022-1037 State v. Hurt
# newpar
# newpar_block = sp who:::"#EricLevi" whom:::"#MichaelPDonnelly,#OtherJustices,#RPatrickDeWine" (24 s)
