# meta::summary1 = (human1) In a letter to Sir de Villiers Graaff, Nelson Mandela expresses concern over the Verwoerd Government's plan to establish a Republic without the consent of the non-white majority in South Africa, and proposes a National Convention to create a new constitution, calling on Graaff to support this initiative.
# meta::summary2 = (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022) In May 1961, Nelson Mandela writes an urgent letter to Opposition Leader Sir de Villiers Graaff, urging him and his United Party to support the call for an all-races National Convention to create a new constitution before the Verwoerd Government inaugurates its Republic against non-white majority wishes.
# meta::summary3 = (gpt4o) In a letter to Sir de Villiers Graaff, Nelson Mandela urges support for a National Convention involving all racial groups in South Africa to prevent a crisis instigated by the Verwoerd Government's minority decision to establish a Republic, emphasizing the importance of unity and democratic consultation in forming a new constitution for lasting peace and stability.
# meta::summary4 = (Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct) The letter, written by Nelson Mandela, urges the Leader of the Opposition, Sir de Villiers Graaff, to support a National Convention to address the crisis in South Africa and prevent civil war, warning that silence and inaction will lead to disaster.
# meta::summary5 = (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) Nelson Mandela urges Sir de Villiers Graaff, the Leader of the Opposition in South Africa, to support a call for a National Convention to address the political crisis caused by the Verwoerd government's plans for a republic, emphasizing the importance of a democratic and peaceful solution that includes all racial groups.
# meta::title = Letter from Nelson Mandela to Sir de Villiers Graaff, leader of the United Party, 23 May 1961
